<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Section 4.4 &amp; 4.5: Finding Angles</title>

    <p>
        <alert>MTH 124</alert>
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            \cos(\theta)=x \iff \arccos(x)=\theta, \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \  \ \theta \text{ in } [0,\pi]
        </md>
        <md>
            \sin(\theta) = y \iff \arcsin(y)=\theta, \ \ \ \ \ \ \theta \text{ in } \left[-\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{\pi}{2}\right]
        </md>
        <md>
            \tan(\theta)=z \iff \arctan(z)=\theta,  \ \ \ \ \ \ \theta \text{ in } \left(-\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{\pi}{2}\right)
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        <term>6 trig functions:</term>
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\sin(\theta)</m>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\cos(\theta)</m>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\tan(\theta)=\frac{\sin(\theta)}{\cos(\theta)}</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\csc(\theta)=\frac{1}{\sin(\theta)} </m>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\sec(\theta)=\frac{1}{\cos(\theta)} </m>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\cot(\theta)=\frac{\cos(\theta)}{\sin(\theta)} = \frac{1}{\tan(\theta)}</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </sidebyside>
<sidebyside widths="10% 65%" margins="0% 25%">
    <p>
        <m>\csc(x):</m>
    </p>

    <image >
      <shortdescription>
        The graph of cosecant.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-4-0a">
      
        <diagram dimensions="(300,150)" margins="5">
          <definition>f(x)=csc(x)</definition>
          <definition>g(x)=sin(x)</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-2*pi-1,-4,2*pi+1,4)">
            <grid-axes  h-pi-format="yes" spacings="((-2*pi,pi/2,2*pi),(-4,1,4))" hlabels="(-2*pi,pi,3*pi)"/>
            <graph function="f" />
            <graph function="g" dash="4 4"/>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    </sidebyside>

    <sidebyside widths="10% 65%" margins="0% 25%">
    <p>
        <m>\sec(x):</m>
    </p>

    <image >
      <shortdescription>
        The graph of secant.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-4-0b">
      
        <diagram dimensions="(300,150)" margins="5">
          <definition>f(x)=sec(x)</definition>
          <definition>g(x)=cos(x)</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-2*pi-1,-4,2*pi+1,4)">
            <grid-axes  h-pi-format="yes" spacings="((-2*pi,pi/2,2*pi),(-4,1,4))" hlabels="(-2*pi,pi,3*pi)"/>
            <graph function="f" />
            <graph function="g" dash="4 4"/>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    </sidebyside>
    <activity>
        <title>Activity 4.4.2. More Practice</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                For each of the following different scenarios, draw a picture of
                the situation and use inverse trigonometric functions
                appropriately to determine the missing information both exactly
                and approximately.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider an angle <m>\alpha</m> in standard position on the
                    unit circle (vertex at the origin, one side on the positive
                    <m>x</m>-axis (making the angle more than
                    <m>90^{\circ} = \frac{\pi}{2}</m> radians)) for which we
                    know <m>\cos(\alpha)=-\frac12</m> and <m>\alpha</m> lies in
                    quadrant III.
                    What is the measure of <m>\alpha</m> in radians?
                    In addition, what is the value of <m>\sin(\alpha)</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S4-4-soln">
              <p>
                In quadrant III, since <m>\cos(\alpha)=-\frac12</m> then the 
                x-coordinate has to be <m>-\frac12</m>, which corresponds to the 
                point <m>(-\frac12, -\frac{\sqrt3}{2})</m>.  This happens when 
                <m>\alpha=\frac{4\pi}{3}</m>, and <m>\sin(\frac{4\pi}{3}) = -\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</m>.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider an angle <m>\beta</m> in standard position on the
                    unit circle for which we know <m>\sin(\beta)=0.1</m> and
                    <m>\beta</m> lies in quadrant II.
                    What is the measure of <m>\beta</m> in radians?
                    In addition, what is the value of <m>\cos(\beta)</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S4-4-soln">
              <p>
                Ideally we would just take <m>\arcsin(0.1)</m> and get out our angle, 
                but that will be about <m>0.100167</m> radians and will land us in quadrant I, 
                not quadrant II, since arcsine gives us angles between <m>-\frac\pi2</m> and 
                <m>\frac{\pi}2</m>.  The nice thing though, is the unit circle is extremely symmetric, 
                so <m>arcsin(0.1)</m> is the angle starting at 0 and going a little bit counterclockwise
                along the unit circle. If we reflect the point over the y-axis into quadrant II, 
                then it would be exactly the same as starting at angle <m>\pi</m> and going backwards by that 
                same amount, or in other words <m>{\beta = \pi - \arcsin(0.1)}</m>.
              </p>
              <p>
                If we tried to take <m>\cos(\arcsin(0.1))</m>, it is about 0.9949, 
                which would put us in the first quadrant!  The value will be the same, but 
                negative since we are in the second quadrant where cosine/x-coordinate of the unit circle is negative, 
                so <m>{\cos(\beta)\approx -0.9949}</m>.  See picture with a slightly exaggerated angle to see the symmetry.
              </p>
                  <image width="35%">
      <shortdescription>
        The graph of the unit circle showing the symmetry.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-4-2bsoln">
      
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
          <definition>beta= pi - asin(0.3)</definition>
          <definition>gamma= asin(0.3)</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,1,1)">
            <axes hlabels="(2,2,2)" vlabels="(2,2,2)" />
            <circle center="(0,0)" radius="1"/>
            <line dash="2 2" endpoints="((0,0),(cos(beta),sin(beta)))"/>
            <line dash="2 2" endpoints="((0,0),(cos(gamma),sin(gamma)))"/>
            <angle-marker points="((cos(beta),sin(beta)),(0,0),(-1,0))"  clear-background="yes">   </angle-marker>
            <angle-marker points="((1,0),(0,0),(cos(gamma),sin(gamma)))"  clear-background="yes">   </angle-marker>
            <angle-marker points="((1,0),(0,0),(cos(beta),sin(beta)))" clear-background="no" alignment="ne"><m>\beta</m>   </angle-marker>
            <point p="(cos(gamma), sin(gamma))" alignment="nw"><m>(x, 0.1)</m></point>
            <point p="(cos(beta), sin(beta))" alignment="ne"><m>(-x, 0.1)</m></point>

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider a right triangle with legs of length <m>11</m> and
                    <m>13</m>.
                    What are the measures (in radians) of the non-right angles
                    and what is the length of the hypotenuse?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S4-4-soln">
              <p>
                Pythagorean Theorem: <m>{11^2 + 13^2 = (hyp)^2}</m> so <m>{hyp=\sqrt{290}}</m>.
              </p>
              <p>
                Pick an angle, I'll call mine <m>\theta</m>, and I will make 
                <m>\theta</m> adjacent to the side of 13 and opposite to the side of 11.
                Since <m>{\tan(\theta)=\frac{11}{13}}</m> then <m>{\theta=\arctan(\frac{11}{13})}</m>.
              </p>
              <p>
                The other angle is <m>\frac\pi2 - \arctan(\frac{11}{13})</m>, or 
                if you like this is actually the same thing as <m>\arctan(\frac{13}{11})</m> 
                if you just take the tangent with respect to this final angle.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <p workspace="0.01in">
        <term>Now let's look at a few applications...</term>
    </p>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 4.4.3</title>

        <statement>
            <p workspace="1in">
                A roof is being built with a ``7-12 pitch.” This means that the
                roof rises 7 inches vertically for every 12 inches of horizontal
                span; in other words, the slope of the roof is
                <m>\frac{7}{12}</m>.
                What is the exact measure (in degrees) of the angle the roof
                makes with the horizontal?
                What is the approximate measure?
                What are the exact and approximate measures of the angle at the
                peak of the roof (made by the front and back portions of the
                roof that meet to form the ridge)?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="S4-4-soln">
              <p>
                I have a right triangle with a horizontal leg of 12, a vertical leg of 7, 
                and I called the angle <m>\theta</m> as the one adjacent to the side of 12.
                So <m>{\tan(\theta) = \frac{7}{12}}</m>, and so <m>{\theta=\arctan(\frac{7}{12})}</m>. 
                If the angle at the top of the triangle is <m>\beta</m>, we can find 
                it many ways, but one of the simplest is <m>\tan(\beta)=\frac{12}{7}</m>
                so <m>\beta=\arctan(\frac{12}{7})</m>.  This is only half of the angle 
                of the roof though, since the roof will rise with this slope but then 
                will fall with this same slope, so there is a mirror image of this triangle as if you 
                reflect over the vertical leg.  So the peak is actually <m>2\beta = 2\arctan(\frac{12}{7})</m>.
              </p>
                  <image width="45%">
      <shortdescription>
        The triangles representing the roof.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-4-3bsoln">
      
        <diagram dimensions="(300,150)" margins="5">

          <coordinates bbox="(-1,0,1,1)">
            <triangle vertices="((-1,0),(1,0),(0,1))"/>

            <line  endpoints="((0,0),(0,1))"/>
            <angle-marker points="((0,0),(-1,0),(0,1))"  clear-background="no"> <m>\theta</m>  </angle-marker>
            <angle-marker points="((0,1),(1,0),(0,0))"  clear-background="no"> <m>\theta</m>  </angle-marker>
            <angle-marker points="((-1,0),(0,1),(0,0))"  clear-background="no"> <m>\beta</m>  </angle-marker>
            <angle-marker points="((0,0),(0,1),(1,0))"  clear-background="no"> <m>\beta</m>  </angle-marker>
            <angle-marker points="((1,0),(0,0),(0,1))"  clear-background="no">  </angle-marker>
            <angle-marker points="((0,1),(0,0),(-1,0))"  clear-background="no">  </angle-marker>
            <label anchor="(0,.4)" alignment="e"><m>7</m></label>
            <label anchor="(-0.5,.06)" alignment="e"><m>12</m></label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
            </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 4.4.4</title>

        <statement>
            <p workspace="1in">
                On a baseball diamond (which is a square with 90-foot sides),
                the third baseman fields the ball right on the line from third
                base to home plate and 10 feet away from third base (towards
                home plate).
                When he throws the ball to first base, what angle (in degrees)
                does the line the ball travels make with the first base line?
                What angle does it make with the third base line?
                Draw a well-labeled diagram to support your thinking.
            </p>

            <p workspace="1in">
                What angles arise if he throws the ball to second base instead?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="S4-4-soln">
              <image width="45%">
      <shortdescription>
        The baseball diamond if the third baseman throws to first base.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-4-4asoln">
      
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">

          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,1,1)">
            <line endpoints="((-1,0),(0,1))"/>
            <line endpoints="((0,1),(1,0))"/>
            <line endpoints="((1,0),(0,-1))"/>
            <line endpoints="((0,-1),(-1,0))"/>
            <line  endpoints="((1,0),(-0.8,-0.2))"/>
            <angle-marker points="((-0.8,-0.2),(1,0),(0,-1))"  clear-background="no"> <m>\theta</m>  </angle-marker>
            <angle-marker points="((1,0),(0,-1),(-1,0))"  clear-background="no">  </angle-marker>
            <label anchor="(0.5,-.5)" alignment="se"><m>90</m></label>
            <label anchor="(-0.4,-0.6)" alignment="sw"><m>80</m></label>
            <label anchor="(-0.9,-0.1)" alignment="sw"><m>10</m></label>
            <point p="(-1,0)" alignment="e">third</point>
            <point p="(1,0)" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,0)">first</point>
            <point p="(0,-1)" alignment="n" offset="(0,10)">home</point>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
              <p>
                First part. See image above.  We have <m>{\tan(\theta)=\frac{80}{90}}</m>
                so the angle with the first base line is <m>{\theta=\arctan(\frac{80}{90})}</m> 
                or about <m>41.63^\circ</m>. Since the non-right angles have to 
                add to 90 degrees, the angle with the third-base line is <m>90^\circ - \arctan(\frac{80}{90})</m>
                or about <m>48.4^\circ</m>.
              </p>
              <p>
                Second part.  See image below. Same reasoning as above, <m>{\tan(\theta)=\frac{10}{90}}</m>
                so <m>{\theta=\arctan(\frac{1}{9})\approx6.3^\circ}</m>.  
                The other angle is <m>{90^\circ - \arctan(\frac19)\approx 87.3^\circ}</m>.
              </p>
                  <image width="45%">
      <shortdescription>
        The baseball diamond if the third baseman throws to second base.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-4-4bsoln">
      
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">

          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,1,1)">
            <line endpoints="((-1,0),(0,1))"/>
            <line endpoints="((0,1),(1,0))"/>
            <line endpoints="((1,0),(0,-1))"/>
            <line endpoints="((0,-1),(-1,0))"/>
            <line  endpoints="((0,1),(-0.8,-0.2))"/>
            <angle-marker points="((-1,0),(0,1),(-0.8,-0.2))" offset="(-10,-11)" clear-background="no"> <m>\theta</m>  </angle-marker>
            <angle-marker points="((1,0),(0,-1),(-1,0))"  clear-background="no">  </angle-marker>
            <label anchor="(-0.5,.5)" alignment="nw"><m>90</m></label>
            <label anchor="(-0.9,-0.1)" alignment="sw"><m>10</m></label>
            <point p="(-1,0)" alignment="e">third</point>
            <point p="(1,0)" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,0)">first</point>
            <point p="(0,-1)" alignment="n" offset="(0,10)">home</point>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
            </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 4.4.5</title>

        <statement>
            <p workspace="1in">
                A camera is tracking the launch of a rocket.
                The camera is located 4000 feet from the rocket’s launching pad,
                and the camera angle changes in order to keep the rocket in
                focus.
                At what angle <m>\theta</m> (in radians) is the camera tilted
                when the rocket is 3000 feet off the ground?
                Answer both exactly and approximately.
            </p>

            <p workspace="1in">
                Now, rather than considering the rocket at a fixed height of
                3000 feet, let its height vary and call the rocket’s height
                <m>h</m>.
                Determine the camera’s angle, <m>\theta</m>, as a function of
                <m>h</m>, (so <m>\theta = ...</m> and <m>h</m> will show up on
                the right), and use Desmos to help compute the average rate of
                change of <m>\theta</m> on the intervals <m>[3000,3500]</m>,
                <m>[5000,5500]</m>, and <m>[7000,7500]</m>.
                What do you observe about how the camera angle is changing?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="S4-4-soln">
              <image width="45%">
      <shortdescription>
        The triangle representing the rocket launch.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-4-5soln">
      
        <diagram dimensions="(300,100)" margins="15">

          <coordinates bbox="(-1,0,1,1)">
            <triangle vertices="((-1,0),(1,0),(1,1))" />
            <angle-marker points="((1,0),(-1,0),(1,1))" offset="(10,3)" clear-background="no"> <m>\theta</m>  </angle-marker>
            <angle-marker points="((1,1),(1,0),(-1,0))"  clear-background="no">  </angle-marker>
            <label anchor="(0,0)" alignment="n"><m>4000</m>ft</label>
            <label anchor="(1,0.5)" alignment="w"><m>h</m></label>
            <point p="(-1,0)" alignment="ne" offset="(0,10)">camera</point>
            <point p="(1,1)" alignment="sw" offset="(-10,4)">rocket</point>
            <point p="(1,0)" alignment="sw" offset="(-0,2)">launch</point>

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
              <p>
                First part.  Take the height <m>h=3000</m> in the image above.
                Then <m>{\tan(\theta)= \frac{3000}{4000}}</m>, and <m>{\theta=\arctan(\frac34)approx 0.6345}</m> radians.
              </p>
              <p>
                Second part.  Same picture but leave <m>h</m> as <m>h</m>.  So 
                Then <m>{\tan(\theta)= \frac{h}{4000}}</m>, and <m>{\theta=\arctan(\frac{h}{4})}</m> radians.
              </p>
              <p>
                If we define a function <m>\theta(h)=\arctan(\frac{h}{4})</m>, then we go back
                to the average rate of change formula:
                <md>
                  AV_{[3000,3500]}=\frac{\theta(3500)-\theta(3000)}{3500-3000} \approx 0.00015 \frac{radians}{foot}
                </md>
                <md>
                  AV_{[5000,5500]}=\frac{\theta(5500)-\theta(5000)}{5500-5000} \approx 0.000092 \frac{radians}{foot}
                </md>
                <md>
                  AV_{[7000,7500]}=\frac{\theta(7500)-\theta(7000)}{7500-7000} \approx 0.000058 \frac{radians}{foot}
                </md>
                so it seems to be slowing down from the camera's perspective.
              </p>
            </solution>
    </activity>
</handout>